[THOUGHT] The D-dimer result was not provided — likely because it wasn’t retrieved or isn’t available in this environment. I must now pivot to an available, high-yield test. Since D-dimer is unavailable or unreported, the next best step is point-of-care or standard clinical evaluation that can rapidly support or refute PE or pericarditis. A 12-lead ECG is fast, non-invasive, and highly informative:  
- Sinus tachycardia is common in PE;  
- S1Q3T3 pattern (though insensitive) or new RBBB supports PE;  
- Diffuse ST elevations (concave up), PR depression, or Spodick’s sign favor pericarditis;  
- ST-segment changes in ACS would be regional and convex.  
ECG is universally available, low-cost, and immediately actionable.

